The key obstruction is now clear. The NP-completeness proof preserves hardness only from a pre-existing planar-girth-9 `(0,1)` instance; it does not make answer-first random instances hard. Conversely, the explicit non-colourable gadget in Section 4 has a short counting refutation, but its core is fixed, so relabelings are duplicates under the required canonical notion and decorated copies are recognizable by ordinary pattern matching. I’m quantifying the natural inverse-generation route and the Track-B compression gap before recording the Step-0 decision.
